  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is a cleaning station?",
      "a": "A cleaning station is a spot on a reef, often a rock or coral head, where cleaner fish or shrimp live and work. Other fish visit and wait their turn to be cleaned, like customers at a shop.",
      "asks": [
        "Where do fish go to get cleaned?",
        "Do fish queue to be cleaned?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why don't big fish eat cleaner wrasse?",
      "a": "A client gains much more from a healthy cleaner than from one small snack, because parasites cause it harm. The cleaner's stripes and dance mark it as a cleaner, and clients signal they are ready, not hunting.",
      "asks": [
        "Why don't groupers eat cleaner fish?",
        "Are cleaner fish safe from predators?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is mutualism?",
      "a": "Mutualism is a partnership between two different species in which both benefit. Cleaner fish and their clients are an example, like bees and flowers or corals and their algae.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a mutual partnership in nature?",
        "Examples of mutualism"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Do cleaner fish ever cheat?",
      "a": "Yes. Cleaner wrasse prefer the client's protective mucus to parasites and sometimes take a bite of it. Clients jolt, chase the cleaner or leave, and cleaners behave better when other fish are watching.",
      "asks": [
        "Do cleaner wrasse bite?",
        "Can cleaners be trusted?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What happens to a reef without cleaner fish?",
      "a": "Experiments that removed cleaner wrasse from small reefs found that fewer fish, and fewer kinds of fish, stayed there, and the remaining fish carried more parasites.",
      "asks": [
        "Why are cleaner fish important?",
        "Do reefs need cleaner wrasse?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "A single cleaner wrasse can carry out more than 2,000 cleaning visits in a day.",
    "Reefs where cleaner wrasse were removed held fewer fish and fewer kinds of fish (Grutter and colleagues, 2003)."
  ],
